<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1966">1966</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Veer Savarkar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vinayak Damodar Savarkar was an Indian politician and ideologue who developed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> nationalist ideology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a> in his 1923 pamphlet Essentials of Hindutva. As a student in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> he was involved with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> House, and in 1910 he was arrested and deported to India, where he was sentenced to 50 years of imprisonment in the Cellular Jail in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andaman-and-nicobar-islands">Andaman and Nicobar Islands</a>. Released in 1924 after a series of mercy petitions, he was restricted to Ratnagiri district until 1937 and then led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a>, which endorsed the idea of India as a Hindu Rashtra. In 1948 he was tried over the conspiracy to assassinate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> but was acquitted for lack of sufficient evidence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vinayak-damodar-savarkar.jpg" alt="Vinayak Damodar Savarkar" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swatyantra_Vir_Savarkar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Savarkar was born on 28 May 1883 into a Marathi Hindu Chitpavan Brahmin family in Bhagur, a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nasik</a> district of the Bombay Presidency. He began his activism as a high school student. In 1903 he and his brother Ganesh founded the Mitra Mela, an underground revolutionary organisation that became the Abhinav Bharat Society in 1906, with the aims of overthrowing British rule and reviving Hindu pride. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bal-gangadhar-tilak">Lokmanya Tilak</a> helped him obtain the Shivaji Scholarship of 1906 for law studies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. There he associated with India House in Highgate and published books advocating complete independence by revolutionary means; British authorities banned his book The Indian War of Independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Arrested in 1910 over his involvement with India House and deported to India, Savarkar was tried before a special tribunal; one charge was abetment of the murder of the Nasik Collector A. M. T. Jackson. In July 1911 he was transported to Port Blair and imprisoned in the Cellular Jail. His first clemency petition, of 30 August 1911, was rejected. He was released on 6 January 1924 after a series of such petitions but was restricted to Ratnagiri District until 1937. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratnagiri">Ratnagiri</a> jail he wrote Essentials of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindutva">Hindutva</a>.</p><p>As president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-mahasabha">Hindu Mahasabha</a>, which endorsed the idea of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as a Hindu Rashtra, Savarkar supported the British war effort in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and criticised the Quit India movement of 1942. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a>'s assassination on 30 January 1948 he was arrested on 5 February and charged with murder, conspiracy and abetment, but he was acquitted for lack of sufficient evidence. The Kapur Commission later concluded that he was involved. On 1 February 1966 he renounced medicines, food and water, and he died on 26 February 1966 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>In Essentials of Hindutva Savarkar described a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> as a patriotic inhabitant of Bharatavarsha, beyond religious identity, and described Hinduism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jainism">Jainism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> as one and the same. He outlined a Hindu Rashtra as Akhand Bharat, a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> across the subcontinent. He opposed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system and untouchability, and ate with dalits in public. Historians including Rachel McDermott, Leonard A. Gordon, Ainslie Embree, Frances Pritchett and Dennis Dalton state that he promoted an anti-Muslim form of Hindu nationalism. He wrote 38 books in English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> and has been called one of the most divisive figures in Indian politics.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vinayak Damodar Savarkar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Hindu Mahasabha officially boycotted the movement, and its president <b>Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</b> wrote a letter, Stick to your Posts, telling Hindu Sabhaites who were members of municipalities, local bodies, legislatures or the army not to join it; later, after requests and persuasion, he chose to join the…</blockquote><a href="/movement/quit-india-movement">Quit India movement · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vinayak Damodar Savarkar</b> and his allies accused him of appeasing Muslims while overlooking their atrocities against Hindus and of allowing the creation of Pakistan.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/gandhism">Gandhism · Critiques &amp; Debates</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Savarkar</b>, an atheist, opposed the caste system: under him the Hindu Mahasabha held all-caste dinners in Nagpur and Kanpur, and he gave parents money so that their children of the so-called lower castes would attend school.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/hindutva">Hindutva · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bhagur, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.88,73.83&amp;year=1936" aria-label="Bhagur on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6542 1428 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Vinayak Damodar Savarkar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6992 1698h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6992 1698h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.88,73.83&amp;year=1936">What was here in 1936? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
